doc for parameterized function of timers. 


explaination of the code:
1) This code will be using the timer2 module to generate a delay

2) I've used a prescaler of 1024, in the CTC mode, with using the ocr2a register for this specific program. This program uses a polling mechanism/ busy-wait loop to read the ofc2a flaf.

3) The math:
prescaler =1024, ocr2a (top): 250,

That means, each tick will be for 64 microseconds, which means if the ocf2a flag is set, 16 milliseconds have passed each time the ocf2a flag is set to 1. 

The parameter passed to the function(n) will be divided by 16, and the whole number of the resulting number is stored and the loop will count down from (n/16) to 0, which means, the loop will run on the condition of "overflows > 0" which fixes off-by-one errors.  


4) The loop counts from "overflows" to the loop condition and the OCF2A flag is polled and the overflows is decremented, if OCF2A is set and it is reset to 0 by writing a logical one to the OCF2A bit. 




Bugs I've come across:


1)The single most impactful bug I've encountered when trying to write this particular function which was parameterized, was when I was attempting to deal with the precision of the sleep function. 

When using the above math, there is a 16 ms precision error. I attempted to deal it with the inaccuracy using a logic where I would divide the number milliseconds(the parameter of the function, 'n') using modular division:

by doing: int y = n % 16
int x = (y /16) * 250

which is implicitly wrong because anything % 16 gives us an output of 0-15, and when diving that with 16, it will result in zero(because the numbers are stored in integers), which effectively makes my logic in the code ineffective and is basically a dead code. 


I've removed all the residua logic and only kept the parameterized function part and I've not made any revisions to make the code perfectly accurate.


2) Fixed the way i used to set the GPIO registers in the last code, by only manipulating that specific bit with which i am concerned, it wasn't a problem in the last code but this is a generally more hygienic practice to follow.



3) It isn't really a BUG, just something I wanted to talk about but, the lines where i disable the timer by clearing the CS bits or writing to PRTIM2 bit of PRR register isn't really required, but it is a hygienic practice. 



4) Another big "MAKE A NOTE" for this code is that there will always be an inaccuracy, but under the range of 16ms.
